- version: 0.4.0
  changes:
  - Program Simulation's playback stops at a connected Time input rather than pausing whenever it changes: while paused the time still moves the robot, Play runs from where the robot is (from the start if it is already there) and Stop returns to it. Without a connected time, playback runs to the end of the program.
  - The playback speed slider doubles from 0.25 to 128 times, with reverse speeds down to -4 times, and shows the current speed.
  - The multi-part pick and place example uses Create Program's Collisions and Environment inputs.

- version: 0.3.6
  changes:
  - Programs avoiding collisions route a Pick and Place's joint moves around obstacles that no turn or lift clears, such as tall fixtures, with RRT-Connect in joint space. Routes are shortened to a few joint waypoints, keep the arm's configuration at the target they lead to, and are the same each time the program is made. Later pick and places try joining the routes found before, as they usually go around the same obstacles.
  - Pick and places are lifted up to 1000 mm, rather than 300 mm, stopping once the lifted points are out of reach, and first try what cleared the previous pick and place, so neighbouring parts move alike.
  - Programs avoiding collisions are made several times faster: the programs tried while planning skip the robot code and are sampled every 5 mm, while the program made checks its motions at its own step size.

- version: 0.3.5
  changes:
  - Added cell collision checks, written in managed code with the convex hulls of the robot's links, the tool, the parts and each separate piece of the environment, so they also run on macOS: the tool and any held part against the robot's own arm, and the arm, tool and held part against the environment and the other parts, where they wait and where they were placed. A pick and place cell checks in tens of milliseconds.
  - Create Program has Collisions and Environment inputs. With Collisions on, each Pick and Place is made again with the tool turned 180 degrees or moving higher between the pick and place if that clears it of collisions and singularities, preferring linear moves that do not flip the arm, with a warning saying so; other targets are kept, and each collision left is an error.
  - Pick and Place has a Symmetric input for parts that look the same turned 180 degrees, which a program avoiding collisions may then place turned from how they were picked.
  - Added the Cell Collisions component, which lists every target with a collision, what collided and the meshes involved.
  - Check Collisions is faster: a fixed environment is checked piece by piece, skipping meshes too far apart to collide.

- version: 0.3.4
  changes:
  - Added the Reachability component, which scores how well a 6-axis robot reaches points with the tool turned through several rotations, away from the J5 = 0 wrist flip boundary, as a colored map with a legend.

- version: 0.3.3
  changes:
  - Programs plan much faster on FANUC CRX robots, around 60 times for a 100-part pick and place: samples along a path are solved by continuation from the previous sample, falling back to the full inverse kinematics near singularities or for large steps, with the same results.
  - Fanuc programs warn when the robot base is not at the world origin but Cartesian targets use the default frame, as their TP positions would be offset by the base.
  - Pick and Place only forces the template's configuration on its joint motions.
  - Pick and Place has an Open input for the opening before a pick and when letting go, to clear neighbouring parts.

- version: 0.3.2
  changes:
  - Load Robot System has an optional Joint Limits input that narrows the robot arm's joint ranges in order, in library degrees; joints after the last limit, or given a single number such as -1, keep their full range. Inverse kinematics only picks solutions within them, targets outside them are errors, and Stream Joints refuses joints outside them.
  - Check Collisions has a Parts input to leave parts held by Set Gripper out of the tool's collision mesh.
  - Fixed "parameter chunk is missing" errors when opening files saved before a component gained an input or output.
  - Added a pick and place example with several parts.

- version: 0.3.1
  changes:
  - Updated to Robots 2.4.4, which fixes loading continuously rotating external axes with unbounded joint limits.
  - The Schunk WSG 50 gripper tool of the pick and place example now ships with the plug-in, as WSG50 in Load Tool.

- version: 0.3.0
  changes:
  - Tools can have gripper jaws that open and close in the simulation and collision checks. Library tools define them with a Jaws element and Jaw layers in the 3dm file.
  - Added the Set Gripper command, which sets the jaw opening and can pick up a part that the tool carries until the next Set Gripper. It only affects the simulation and adds no code to the program. Program Simulation shows the parts in its new Parts output, and a held part counts as part of the tool in collision checks.
  - Added the Pick and Place component, which creates a pick and place from pick and place planes and a template target, with approach and retreat distances, a grip opening and an optional part.
  - The CRX meshes now keep FANUC's colors.
  - Fixed Fanuc programs writing an empty line for commands without code.
  - Added a pick and place example definition, with a Schunk WSG 50 gripper tool.

- version: 0.2.0
  changes:
  - Added the Stream Joints component, which makes a FANUC robot follow joints that change while it moves, such as from sliders or a solver, smoothed to stay within the controller's limits. Joints more than 20 deg from the robot are refused.
  - The components are now in the RobotsCRX Grasshopper tab. Existing definitions still open, as components are identified by ID.
  - RobotsCRX warns at startup when the Robots package or another copy of Robots is also installed, as only one of them can load.

- version: 0.1.0
  changes:
  - First RobotsCRX release, based on Robots 2.4.3 by Vicente Soler (visose).
  - Added FANUC CRX robots (CRX-3iA, CRX-5iA, CRX-10iA, CRX-10iA/L, CRX-10iA/L Paint, CRX-20iA/L, CRX-30iA), bundled with the plug-in and converted from FANUC’s fanuc_description (Apache-2.0).
  - Fanuc robots now choose the inverse kinematics solver from their geometry, so CRX arms use the non-spherical wrist solver.
  - Added streaming to FANUC controllers with Stream Motion (J519) through the Remote Connection component.
  - Added the Read Joints component to read the current joints from the controller.
  - When Stream Motion is not ready, the error links FANUC’s controller setup checks.
  - Fixed inverse kinematics giving up on some reachable non-spherical wrist targets.
  - Fixed saving the local library folder when Robots is not installed through the package manager.
  - Ships the Robots license and notices, and FANUC’s Apache-2.0 license and notice for the CRX meshes.

- version: 2.4.4
  changes:
  - Fixed loading continuously rotating external axes with unbounded joint limits.

- version: 2.4.3
  changes:
  - Fixed inverse kinematics near wrist singularities.
  - Fixed fly-by simulation in moving frames and multi-axis track offsets.
  - Fixed FANUC tool and frame selection between targets and program files.
  - Preserved KUKA external-axis values when custom overrides are blank or missing.
  - Fixed Cartesian joint motion output for Frankx.
  - Prevented case-insensitive attribute-name collisions in ABB and KUKA programs.
  - Added validation of robot-library joint speeds, geometry, ranges, and numbering.

- version: 2.4.2
  changes:
  - Fixed linear-motion verification starting from the destination pose instead of the previous pose.
  - Fixed simulation and collision checking choosing inconsistent inverse-kinematics branches along verified paths, including ABB PoWa paths.
  - Added verification of simulated fly-by curves so motion errors are reported by Create Program and prevent code generation.
  - Fixed intermediate motion checks being skipped when external axes or coupled robots move while the TCP stays stationary.

- version: 2.4.1
  changes:
  - Improved inverse-kinematics performance for non-spherical wrists, including ABB PoWa and GoFa, especially along continuous paths and when a configuration is specified.
  - Improved Create Program performance by avoiding redundant endpoint solves during motion verification.
  - Fixed Kinematics outputting the previous robot pose when a target could not be solved.

- version: 2.4.0
  changes:
  - Added analytical IK for non-spherical wrists (including ABB PoWa/GoFa) and fixed-redundancy seven-axis arms (including Franka Emika Panda).
  - Added optional `franky` 2 output for Franka, with continuous Process paths.
  - Added optional library `postProcessor`/`solver` selection and custom postprocessor control over commands, validation and saved files; fixed declaration-only custom commands.
  - Added UR runtime frames; fixed small rotations and URP XML escaping.
  - Fixed ABB negative configuration quadrants, KUKA axis speeds/external declarations, Doosan multi-file declaration ordering, and Staubli XYZ conversion/input waits.
  - Fixed Igus joint conversion, digital-output mapping and numbering; Jaka Cartesian joint motions/analogue output; and both robots' Cartesian frame transforms.
  - Corrected numerical IK orientation checks, non-spherical-wrist linear-path configurations, joint ordering and degenerate TCP calibration.
  - Corrected rotation timing/path checks, simulated waits, stationary-program collision checks and external-axis sampling units.
  - Preserved Grasshopper data/wires on upgrade; fixed multi-robot previous joints, culture-dependent joint text, wire previews and metal-forming sample indexing; bounded pose caching.
  - Fixed quiet-command cancellation on Franka and remote log updates repeating actions.
  - Preserved offline access and installed libraries after failed downloads; fixed saved selections, scrolling and settings-error handling.
  - Improved speed/zone lookup, prevented attribute-name collisions, and added early validation of manufacturer mismatches and unsupported ABB controller-numbered I/O.

- version: 2.3.0
  changes:
  - Added a speed-proportional analogue-output motion command with ABB RAPID support.
  - Fixed ABB OmniCore multi-file programs referencing `.MOD` files instead of the saved `.modx` files.
  - Updated SSH.NET to 2026.0.0 to address known security vulnerabilities.

- version: 2.2.5
  changes:
  - Fixed ABB remote commands looking for the helper executable in Rhino's runtime folder.

- version: 2.2.4
  changes:
  - Fixed ABB saved programs failing to load when generated `.pgf` files used LF-only line endings.

- version: 2.2.3
  changes:
  - Improved Create Program performance for large fly-by toolpaths.
  - Kept fly-by zones on targets with commands and warn when command timing may not match exact target positions.
  - Removed unnecessary `robot 0` text from warnings and errors in single-robot programs.

- version: 2.2.2
  changes:
  - Fixed slow Create Program solves caused by repeated motion warnings in large toolpaths.
  - Improved Create Program performance when many targets use unnamed custom speeds, zones, frames, tools, or commands.

- version: 2.2.1
  changes:
  - Fixed program simulation jumping to the wrong pose when the time input jumps directly to the end and the last target has a fly-by zone.
  - Added warnings when a fly-by zone is larger than half of an adjacent segment and simulation caps the effective blend distance.

- version: 2.2.0
  changes:
  - Added geometric fly-by motion samples while keeping command-bearing targets as stop points.
  - Added optional robot library collision meshes for mechanisms and tools, with display meshes used as the fallback.
  - Added FANUC tool and frame number output with validation for controller-managed tool and frame data.
  - Improved collision checking validation, endpoint coverage, and deterministic collision selection.
  - Added helpers and validation for custom external-axis values.
  - Added warnings when acceleration settings make velocity-only program timing optimistic.
  - Expanded the wiki with workflow, component, parameter, manufacturer, and troubleshooting guidance, and changed plugin Help menu items to open the relevant wiki section in the system browser.

- version: 2.1.2
  changes:
  - Fixed raw Target objects returned from Grasshopper Python outputs being treated as recursive lists.

- version: 2.1.1
  changes:
  - Changed the Rhino package to target Rhino 8.21 or newer.
  - Fixed older Create Program components failing after opening in newer versions.

- version: 2.1.0
  changes:
  - Added UR Process motion output through `movep` and exposed Process in the Create Target motion list.
  - Added explicit Grasshopper persistence for Frame, Speed, and Zone parameters and safer value duplication for persistent Robots parameters.
  - Changed Grasshopper Command, Target, Tool, and Toolpath parameters to be volatile runtime data instead of unsupported persistent data.
  - Changed Grasshopper preview toggles to appear only for preview-capable Robots objects.
  - Changed robot targets, target attributes, toolpaths, and command groups to use init-only or read-only public APIs.
  - Changed `Command.RunBefore` to init-only.
  - Changed public API to reduce unused surface area.
  - Fixed robot library XML quaternions that are not normalized, matching Rhino's previous behavior.
  - Fixed Grasshopper value-list placement when adding Create Target inputs.
  - Fixed robot library help links opening from Grasshopper.
  - Fixed KUKA external-axis speed output.

- version: 2.0.0
  changes:
  - Dropped Rhino 7 and .NET Framework plug-in builds; this release targets Rhino 8 on .NET 8.
  - "Breaking API changes: modernized the core library, package layout, collections, and serialization."
  - Removed bundled ABB PC SDK and Newtonsoft.Json DLLs. ABB remote control now runs through a .NET 10 out-of-process helper that loads ABB PC SDK 2026 at runtime.
  - Existing Grasshopper definitions using supported components should continue to open; obsolete components now show clearer migration messages.
  - Added Cartesian joint motions for robots using numerical IK.
  - Added a RhinoCommon developer package that avoids copying Robots runtime assemblies to consumer plug-ins.
  - Simplified `FileIO.LoadRobotSystem` defaults for loading meshes and using the default post processor.
  - Updated non-Rhino build, test, and sample projects to .NET 10.
  - Reorganized icons, package resources, samples, and documentation assets.
  - Moved package metadata out of root build defaults.
  - Fixed program-checking issues around numerical IK, external axes, undefined configurations, and invalid target states.
  - Fixed error reporting so invalid robot libraries fail early and robot program errors are collected consistently.
  - Fixed Message command escaping for ABB, KUKA, UR, Staubli, Franka, Doosan, Fanuc, Igus, and Jaka.
  - Fixed KUKA Cartesian joint motions to include status and turn data.
  - Fixed unsupported external axes, command declarations, and frame coupling to report program errors on Igus, Jaka, and Staubli.
  - Other bug fixes, validation improvements, and cleanup.

